    "id": "australian-mp-s-condition-improves-after-c68a9444",
    "title": "Australian MP's condition improves after alleged car attack",
    "titleJa": "Australian MP's condition improves after alleged car attack",
    "summaryJa": "The independent MP is now in a serious but stable condition, according to the Melbourne hospital.",
    "bodyOriginal": "Australian MP's condition improves after alleged car attack\n- Published\nThe condition of Helen Haines, the Australian MP hit by a car five days ago in north-east Victoria, has improved from critical to serious but stable.\nThe independent MP, 65, had both her legs amputated after what authorities say was a deliberate attack outside her electorate office in Wangaratta, north-east of Melbourne, on Monday.\nIn the latest update on Saturday morning, the Alfred Hospital in Melbourne said Haines was in a serious but stable condition, the first improvement since she was airlifted there.\nHer alleged attacker, 43-year-old Brendan Nugent, was arrested shortly after the incident and has since appeared in court on three charges.\nThe politician's husband, Phil Haines, said on Wednesday that the \"horrendous incident\" had caused his wife significant injuries but \"her heart and her mind\" were \"in good shape\".\nNugent, who was arrested moments after he allegedly drove his Toyota LandCruiser into the MP, appeared in court to face three charges: dangerous driving causing serious injury, intentionally causing serious injury, and causing harm to a Commonwealth public official.\nPolice said more charges might be laid, including attempted murder.\nHis lawyer said his client had \"mental health concerns\" but did not give any further detail.\nVictoria Police has said its investigation, with support from the Australian Federal Police, remains ongoing and a motive is yet to be established.\nInvestigators are looking into whether drug use or medication played a part in the incident.\nNugent's family has said they were long-time friends with Haines.\nShortly after the alleged attack, Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese told ABC television that there had been a \"rise in temperature\" and \"too much shouting, not enough discourse\" in Australia.\nHaines, known for stances such as supporting stronger climate action, entered parliament in 2019 after working as a nurse and midwife.\n- Published2 days ago\n- Published3 days ago",
    "bodyJa": "Australian MP's condition improves after alleged car attack\n- Published\nThe condition of Helen Haines, the Australian MP hit by a car five days ago in north-east Victoria, has improved from critical to serious but stable.\nThe independent MP, 65, had both her legs amputated after what authorities say was a deliberate attack outside her electorate office in Wangaratta, north-east of Melbourne, on Monday.\nIn the latest update on Saturday morning, the Alfred Hospital in Melbourne said Haines was in a serious but stable condition, the first improvement since she was airlifted there.\nHer alleged attacker, 43-year-old Brendan Nugent, was arrested shortly after the incident and has since appeared in court on three charges.\nThe politician's husband, Phil Haines, said on Wednesday that the \"horrendous incident\" had caused his wife significant injuries but \"her heart and her mind\" were \"in good shape\".\nNugent, who was arrested moments after he allegedly drove his Toyota LandCruiser into the MP, appeared in court to face three charges: dangerous driving causing serious injury, intentionally causing serious injury, and causing harm to a Commonwealth public official.\nPolice said more charges might be laid, including attempted murder.\nHis lawyer said his client had \"mental health concerns\" but did not give any further detail.\nVictoria Police has said its investigation, with support from the Australian Federal Police, remains ongoing and a motive is yet to be established.\nInvestigators are looking into whether drug use or medication played a part in the incident.\nNugent's family has said they were long-time friends with Haines.\nShortly after the alleged attack, Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese told ABC television that there had been a \"rise in temperature\" and \"too much shouting, not enough discourse\" in Australia.\nHaines, known for stances such as supporting stronger climate action, entered parliament in 2019 after working as a nurse and midwife.\n- Published2 days ago\n- Published3 days ago",
