    "id": "civil-servant-who-juggled-five-jobs-says-83bf228b",
    "title": "Civil servant who juggled five jobs says he was 'surprised' to be charged with fraud",
    "titleJa": "Civil servant who juggled five jobs says he was 'surprised' to be charged with fraud",
    "summaryJa": "Kashim Chowdhury denied being dishonest when not telling employers he had other jobs.",
    "bodyOriginal": "Civil servant who juggled five jobs says he was 'surprised' to be charged with fraud\n- Published\nA civil servant accused of fraudulently juggling multiple government jobs says he was ''surprised'' to have been charged with fraud.\nKashim Chowdhury told the jury at Southwark Crown Court he had not been dishonest in failing to tell his employers he had other government jobs.\n\"At that time I was just on auto pilot, I was like a robot, I was not thinking clearly,\" he said.\nChowdhury, 55, from east London, is accused of nine counts of fraud. He denies acting dishonestly or intending to make a dishonest gain.\nBetween 2020 and 2023 Chowdhury had eight overlapping public sector jobs. He was contractually obliged to work full time and was paid a full-time salary by each organisation. He is accused of fraudulently earning more than £284,000.\nIn March 2023 Chowdhury began working for the Home Office despite being under contract to work full time at the Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (Defra), the UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA), the Department for Business and Trade and the Food Standards Agency.\nThe court was told he started the Home Office job while on sick leave from three of his other jobs.\nProsecuting barrister Lucy Organ asked him: \"If you were really sick for work, how were you able to start your work at the Home Office?\"\nChowdhury said: \"I did not want this job at the Home Office, I was told there would be adequate support and I could work whatever hours I wanted to work.\"\nOrgan also asked why he did not submit a sick note to the Home Office \"You were using these sick notes to be able to cope. It was all too much wasn't it?\"\nChowdhury rejected the suggestion, adding: \"I wanted to carry on working.\"\nIn May 2023, Chowdhury was contracted to work 183 hours a week, more than the number of hours in seven days.\nHe told the jury he agreed with his employers to work flexibly and that his work was project or output based. He said this was agreed \"informally\" via text messages but the phones used to send the texts had gone missing when his house was searched by investigators.\nHe said that to cope with the workload he would work from 07:00 on Saturdays and Sundays and not finish until 20:00 or 21:00.\nCivil servant faked illness with make-up to juggle five jobs, court hears\n- Published14 October\nCivil servant admits he 'made mistakes' juggling five government jobs\n- Published2 days ago\nOrgan asked him: \"Wasn't that a bit much for you?\"\n\"I was house bound, I was looking after my mum,\" he said.\nThe prosecution alleges Chowdhury managed to keep his overlapping jobs secret because he worked entirely remotely. He told his employers he was the sole carer for his elderly mother and the prosecution alleges he \"exploited this opportunity\".\nHis line manager at the UKHSA, Aleesha Adams, organised a video call with him in May 2023 to discuss his sick leave.\nShe previously told the court she was surprised when he logged on wearing white make-up because his name led her to expect to see an Asian man with darker skin.\nShe told the court that as the meeting went on the make-up started coming off his forehead. ''He was sweating, it was like rubbing off so it was darker underneath and darker on the neck.\"\nBut Chowdhury denied having worn make-up at the meeting\n''That's a smear, I've never worn make-up in my life. At that particular time, I was suffering from anxiety and depression. I had already submitted my sick note, there was no need to put on make-up.''\nThe trial continues.",
    "bodyJa": "Civil servant who juggled five jobs says he was 'surprised' to be charged with fraud\n- Published\nA civil servant accused of fraudulently juggling multiple government jobs says he was ''surprised'' to have been charged with fraud.\nKashim Chowdhury told the jury at Southwark Crown Court he had not been dishonest in failing to tell his employers he had other government jobs.\n\"At that time I was just on auto pilot, I was like a robot, I was not thinking clearly,\" he said.\nChowdhury, 55, from east London, is accused of nine counts of fraud. He denies acting dishonestly or intending to make a dishonest gain.\nBetween 2020 and 2023 Chowdhury had eight overlapping public sector jobs. He was contractually obliged to work full time and was paid a full-time salary by each organisation. He is accused of fraudulently earning more than £284,000.\nIn March 2023 Chowdhury began working for the Home Office despite being under contract to work full time at the Department for Environment, Food and Rural Affairs (Defra), the UK Health Security Agency (UKHSA), the Department for Business and Trade and the Food Standards Agency.\nThe court was told he started the Home Office job while on sick leave from three of his other jobs.\nProsecuting barrister Lucy Organ asked him: \"If you were really sick for work, how were you able to start your work at the Home Office?\"\nChowdhury said: \"I did not want this job at the Home Office, I was told there would be adequate support and I could work whatever hours I wanted to work.\"\nOrgan also asked why he did not submit a sick note to the Home Office \"You were using these sick notes to be able to cope. It was all too much wasn't it?\"\nChowdhury rejected the suggestion, adding: \"I wanted to carry on working.\"\nIn May 2023, Chowdhury was contracted to work 183 hours a week, more than the number of hours in seven days.\nHe told the jury he agreed with his employers to work flexibly and that his work was project or output based. He said this was agreed \"informally\" via text messages but the phones used to send the texts had gone missing when his house was searched by investigators.\nHe said that to cope with the workload he would work from 07:00 on Saturdays and Sundays and not finish until 20:00 or 21:00.\nCivil servant faked illness with make-up to juggle five jobs, court hears\n- Published14 October\nCivil servant admits he 'made mistakes' juggling five government jobs\n- Published2 days ago\nOrgan asked him: \"Wasn't that a bit much for you?\"\n\"I was house bound, I was looking after my mum,\" he said.\nThe prosecution alleges Chowdhury managed to keep his overlapping jobs secret because he worked entirely remotely. He told his employers he was the sole carer for his elderly mother and the prosecution alleges he \"exploited this opportunity\".\nHis line manager at the UKHSA, Aleesha Adams, organised a video call with him in May 2023 to discuss his sick leave.\nShe previously told the court she was surprised when he logged on wearing white make-up because his name led her to expect to see an Asian man with darker skin.\nShe told the court that as the meeting went on the make-up started coming off his forehead. ''He was sweating, it was like rubbing off so it was darker underneath and darker on the neck.\"\nBut Chowdhury denied having worn make-up at the meeting\n''That's a smear, I've never worn make-up in my life. At that particular time, I was suffering from anxiety and depression. I had already submitted my sick note, there was no need to put on make-up.''\nThe trial continues.",
